; SmoothFemaleCasual — a new Human look variant (Villager). Import only: wiring this into
; project/data/animals/human.tres's `model_scenes` is gameplay-engineer's step and is NOT
; done here.
;
; Source: Quaternius, "Animated Women Characters" (Feb 2019) pack (FBX), Smooth_Female_Casual.fbx — CC0 1.0
; Universal, confirmed from the pack's own License.txt before import (copied to
; res://assets/licenses/Quaternius_AnimatedWomenCharacters_License.txt).
; NEW attribution entry for this project: project/attribution/sources/quaternius_animated_women_characters.tres.
;
; SMOOTH VARIANT: this is the pack's subdivided twin of Female_Casual (human_female_casual) —
; identical silhouette and identical material set, ~4x the triangles (7240 here). Both
; sets were imported deliberately so the base-vs-smooth comparison can be made
; in-engine; the losing set gets pulled later. That comparison is a human call.
;
; RIG + CLIPS (MEASURED on this file, not assumed): `HumanArmature`, 42 bones, one
; AnimationPlayer with 11 clips —
; HumanArmature|Female_{Idle, Walk, Run, Standing, Sitting, Clapping, Jump, RunningJump,
; Punch, SwordSlash, Death}. NOTE THE PREFIX: the men's pack names its clips
; `HumanArmature|Man_*`, NOT `Male_*` — same convention the already-shipped
; human_man/Man.glb uses, which is why `autoplay` below reads the way it does. The
; women's pack uses `HumanArmature|Female_*`.
; Bone count differs from the shipped Man.glb (42 here vs 31 there) even though the
; armature name and all 11 clip names match exactly — the .glb was exported with
; unskinned/unanimated bones stripped. Recorded, not corrected; no retarget is
; involved either way.
;
; MATERIAL/TEXTURE AUDIT (measured on the raw, unmodified FBX): albedo_texture is null
; on all 8 surfaces, and NO mesh surface carries an ARRAY_COLOR channel, so the
; importer's `vertex_color_use_as_albedo = true` is inert (COLOR defaults to white) and
; color comes entirely from each named material's albedo_color. Every surface has a
; distinct, meaningful color — NOT the uniform ~0.9063 grey tell that flagged the
; DeadTree/PineTree2 missing-texture bug. Nothing to fix; no material_override applied.
; art.md's no-pure-black palette rule was checked per surface across all 13 models in
; this batch: the darkest garment anywhere is linear 0.1652 (~44% sRGB grey), well clear
; of the linear-0.0185 near-black that held Male_Suit/Smooth_Male_Suit out of this batch.
;
; SCALE: 0.214811 — MEASURED, not a judgment call. Raw AABB measured via manual
; local-transform composition (NOT global_transform — see
; project/data/terrain/cultivated_field.tres's header for why that silently returns
; identity on a freshly-instantiated, not-yet-tree-attached node): 4.989902 x 4.655264 x
; 0.957939 (x, y, z). scale = 1 / measured AABB height = 1 / 4.655264 = 0.214811, the same
; rule Man.tscn states (tile height = 1.0 unit; gdd.md -> Level & world design, "a
; villager stands ~1 tile tall"). Scaled height lands on 1.0000.
; CONSEQUENCE WORTH A HUMAN LOOK: the women's raw meshes are ~3.8% shorter than the
; men's (4.6553 vs 4.8413), so per-model 1/height normalization erases that
; difference and makes every villager exactly the same height. If men and women
; should read as different heights, one shared factor (e.g. 0.207 for all 18
; variants) is the alternative. Flagged, not decided.
; The bind pose spreads the arms wide (bbox width 4.9899 pre-scale, ~1.0719 post) —
; a rest-pose artifact, not a height input; the eyeball check should confirm the Idle
; clip resolves to a normal standing pose rather than the bind T-pose. Same note
; Man.tscn carries.
;
; FACING: left as authored (identity transform, no rotation) — unconfirmed by a human
; eye pass. FLAG FOR HUMAN SIGN-OFF.
